── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #2660  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Privilege-mode filtering on cycle/instret counters must be enforced exactly: no increments in inhibited modes, unimplemented-mode bits must be read-only zero, exception-causing instructions must not increment instret, and xRET must only increment if the originating mode is non-inhibited — otherwise execution metadata leaks across privilege boundaries
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:252-254  (top-level defines)
      These lines correctly define CSR_CYCLE (0xc00) and CSR_INSTRET (0xc02) per the RISC-V privileged spec. The defines themselves are correct. The security-relevant observation is the ABSENCE of corresponding mcyclecfg/minstretcfg CSR definitions — those CSR numbers appear nowhere in the entire kernel source tree (confirmed by grep). This absence is a design-level signal that the kernel never programmatically interacts with the Smcntrpmf filtering CSRs, but the #define lines themselves are not buggy.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:284-286  (top-level defines)
      Correctly defines CSR_CYCLEH (0xc80) and CSR_INSTRETH (0xc82) for RV32 upper-32-bit counter access. The defines themselves are correct per spec. The corresponding mcyclecfgh/minstretcfgh CSR numbers are absent from the entire kernel, mirroring the gap at lines 252-254 for 32-bit platforms, but the #define lines are not themselves violations.
  [RISK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu_legacy.c:46-64  pmu_legacy_read_ctr
      Directly reads CSR_CYCLE/CSR_INSTRET (and _H variants on CONFIG_32BIT) via riscv_pmu_ctr_read_csr() with zero awareness of mcyclecfg/minstretcfg INH filter bits. Currently safe because the kernel never writes mcyclecfg/minstretcfg (no definitions exist, no code touches them), so INH bits default to 0 per spec and counters are uninhibited. However, this depends on the implicit invariant that 'no entity (firmware/bootloader) has set INH bits' — an invariant the kernel cannot verify since it can't read mcyclecfg/minstretcfg either. If firmware sets INH bits, the kernel would (a) read counter values whose interpretation is wrong, or (b) if hardware is buggy and still counts in inhibited modes, leak execution metadata.
  [RISK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu.c:91-129  csr_read_num
      Low-level switch-based CSR read dispatch covering CSR_CYCLE..CSR_HPMCOUNTER31H via macro expansion. Performs raw csr_read() with no awareness of mcyclecfg/minstretcfg INH bits. This is the lowest-level read path in the PMU subsystem and is completely Smcntrpmf-unaware. The function dispatches on csr_num and returns whatever the hardware gives — no pre-read check of whether the current privilege mode is inhibited for that counter. Currently safe under the same unenforced invariant as pmu_legacy_read_ctr (INH bits remain 0).
  [RISK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu.c:134-143  riscv_pmu_ctr_read_csr
      Validates CSR number is in [CSR_CYCLE..CSR_HPMCOUNTER31H] range and dispatches to csr_read_num(). The range check is a bounds guard, not an INH filter check. Any caller obtaining a counter CSR number in this range gets a raw hardware read with no privilege-mode filtering awareness. The function has no way to determine whether the read should be inhibited because the kernel has no mcyclecfg/minstretcfg access. The gap is architectural: this is the chokepoint where an INH check should logically live, but none exists.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_pmu.c:819-885  kvm_riscv_vcpu_pmu_init
      The comment at lines 826-828 explicitly states the Smcntrpmf requirement: 'PMU functionality should be only available to guests if privilege mode filtering is available in the host. Otherwise, guest will always count events while the execution is in hypervisor mode.' This correctly identifies that without privilege-mode filtering (Smcntrpmf), the hypervisor's execution time leaks to the guest via counter deltas — a textbook info_leak. But the gate at line 830 checks riscv_isa_extension_available(NULL, SSCOFPMF), not Smcntrpmf. SSCOFPMF provides overflow interrupts, not privilege-mode filtering. Result: (a) a system with Smcntrpmf but without SSCOFPMF incorrectly skips guest PMU (functional loss), and (b) a system with SSCOFPMF but without Smcntrpmf incorrectly enables guest PMU, leaking hypervisor execution timing to the guest — the exact scenario the comment warns about.
  [RISK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu_legacy.c:110-129  pmu_legacy_init
      Registers the legacy PMU with cmask covering only cycle (bit 0) and instret (bit 2), and sets PERF_PMU_CAP_NO_EXCLUDE at line 127 — explicitly admitting inability to support exclude_kernel/exclude_user. The comment block (lines 103-108) acknowledges this is 'just a simple implementation' that will be 'removed in future.' With Smcntrpmf awareness, the kernel could read mcyclecfg/minstretcfg to discover hardware filtering capability and potentially set PERF_PMU_CAP_NO_EXCLUDE conditionally. The current unconditional setting means userspace can never request filtered counting even on hardware that supports it via Smcntrpmf, though the invariant that INH bits remain 0 keeps this from being actively exploitable.
  [OK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu_sbi.c:1192-1199  pmu_sbi_setup_irqs
      Gates PMU IRQ setup (overflow interrupt) on SSCOFPMF availability. This is architecturally correct: Smcntrpmf provides privilege-mode filtering but does NOT provide overflow interrupts — those come from SSCOFPMF. The function's purpose is IRQ configuration, and SSCOFPMF is the correct gate for IRQ support. The absence of an Smcntrpmf check here does not create a vulnerability because Smcntrpmf has no bearing on interrupt capability. Marked compliant for this specific code location; the broader observation that Smcntrpmf is never checked anywhere in initialization remains valid but is captured in other findings.
  [RISK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu_sbi.c:485-509  riscv_pmu_get_hpm_info
      Iterates the SBI PMU counter list to discover hardware counter width/count. Skips CSR_CYCLE and CSR_INSTRET (line 499) when determining hpm_width, treating them as special fixed counters — but does not check whether Smcntrpmf filtering is available for these counters. The function queries SBI firmware for counter metadata; if the firmware is Smcntrpmf-aware and reports counters with filtering capability, that information is silently discarded for cycle/instret. The resulting hpm_width may not reflect the actual filtering capability of cycle/instret counters. Currently low-risk because no downstream consumer acts on filtering capability, but the information loss means future Smcntrpmf-aware code would need to refactor this function.
  [RISK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu_sbi.c:538-574  pmu_sbi_ctr_get_idx
      Uses CSR_CYCLE base for counter matching and passes exclude_kernel/exclude_user filter flags via SBI_EXT_PMU_COUNTER_CFG_MATCH. Relies entirely on SBI firmware (M-mode) to enforce filtering — the kernel itself never touches mcyclecfg/minstretcfg. In the legacy fast path (lines 555-562), forces cmask to bit 0 for cycles or BIT(2) for instret without any INH consideration. If the SBI firmware is Smcntrpmf-aware, this works correctly because firmware sets the INH bits. But if firmware is Smcntrpmf-unaware, the filtering flags (SINH/UINH) are silently ignored, and the counter counts in all modes regardless of exclude_kernel/exclude_user — userspace gets unfiltered deltas that include kernel execution time. The kernel has no fallback to detect that filtering was not actually applied.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_pmu.c:364-397  kvm_riscv_vcpu_pmu_read_hpm
      KVM trap-and-emulate handler for CSR_CYCLE/CSR_INSTRET reads. When SSCOFPMF is absent AND init_done is false (lines 371-384), returns 0 for cycle/instret as legacy compat — this is a safe fallback that prevents the guest from observing any counter value. However, when init_done IS true (PMU was initialized), the read dispatches to pmu_ctr_read() at line 393 which reads a host perf_event counter that may include HS-mode (hypervisor) execution cycles. There is no mcyclecfg/minstretcfg INH-aware emulation. A guest reading cycle/instret gets the raw perf counter value, which — on the path where init succeeded — reflects all privilege modes including HS-mode hypervisor execution between VM exits. This is the runtime manifestation of the bug confirmed at kvm_riscv_vcpu_pmu_init: once the wrong gate lets PMU init proceed, this read path leaks hypervisor timing to the guest.
  [OK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu_legacy.c:20-32  pmu_legacy_ctr_get_idx
      Simple event-to-index mapping: PERF_COUNT_HW_CPU_CYCLES → idx 0, PERF_COUNT_HW_INSTRUCTIONS → idx 2. This is a static mapping with no side effects. The function doesn't read any CSR and doesn't configure filtering, so it can't directly cause a violation. The fixed indexing is deterministic and correct for the legacy PMU design. The Smcntrpmf-unawareness is architectural (the kernel doesn't know whether filtering is available) but this specific function is just an index lookup and is safe as-is.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_vcpu_pmu.h:111-121  kvm_riscv_vcpu_pmu_read_legacy
      CONFIG_RISCV_PMU_SBI=n stub that returns 0 for CSR_CYCLE/CSR_INSTRET reads and KVM_INSN_ILLEGAL_TRAP for others. This is a build-time safety fallback: returning 0 is the safest choice (prevents any counter observation) and `KVM_INSN_ILLEGAL_TRAP` for other CSRs follows the ISA-mandated behavior. The function is unconditionally safe — it never leaks counter values because it always returns 0 for the two counters that exist. Smcntrpmf consideration is irrelevant here because no real counter is ever read on this code path.
  [OK] drivers/perf/Kconfig:124-124  (Kconfig help text)
      The sole mention of 'Smcntrpmf' in the entire kernel source tree is in the Andes custom PMU Kconfig help text: 'similar to the standard Sscofpmf and Smcntrpmf extension.' This is purely documentation — no functional code. It confirms that Smcntrpmf is known to exist as a concept but has zero implementation presence in the kernel. Marked compliant because a Kconfig help string cannot cause a security violation.

